  "summary": "Chav is a pejorative slang term that became widespread in the United Kingdom in the late 1990s and early 2000s to describe a stereotype of a white, working-class youth.",
  "description": "Chav is a pejorative slang term that became widespread in the United Kingdom in the late 1990s and early 2000s to describe a stereotype of a white, working-class youth. The term is associated with a specific aesthetic and a perceived set of behaviors, often relating to anti-social conduct.\n\nThe visual style linked to the stereotype is rooted in sportswear and consumer culture of the era, characterized by items such as tracksuits, puffer jackets, baseball caps, and trainers from brands like Nike and Adidas. Other key elements included an aspirational adoption of luxury brands, most famously the Burberry check pattern (often in counterfeit form), and bold jewelry like large gold hoop earrings and chains. While the term is sometimes used to describe a genuine youth subculture, it is widely recognized and criticized as a classist slur used to mock and denigrate working-class people.",
  "culturalContext": "Key values (per the Aesthetics Wiki): Working-class youth culture, brand aspiration, consumerism, street style",
